USB/IP lets a USB device plugged into one machine be used by another
over the network, as though it were plugged in there.

This is a from-scratch BSD-licensed implementation for FreeBSD of both
halves of it, interoperating with the Linux tools at either end.

vhci(4) is a virtual USB host controller: devices exported by a remote
host appear on the local USB bus and ordinary class drivers attach to
them.  usbip(8) drives it - list, attach, detach.

usbipd(8) is the other direction, exporting local devices.  It runs
entirely in userland on top of libusb and ugen(4), so nothing has to be
loaded into the kernel to export a device.

Control, bulk, interrupt and isochronous transfers are all carried.
